---
/** /about for UK towns (Glasgow Phase 2): static, hand-written, never passes through the content
 *  pipeline. Same honesty rules as the US page: say plainly that this is an automated site run by one
 *  person, and disclose the sister sites from day one. */
import { siteConfig, ALL_SITES } from '../../../lib/site-config';
import { aboutSourcesFor } from '../../../lib/about-sources';

const sources = aboutSourcesFor(siteConfig.townId);
const siblingSites = ALL_SITES.filter((s) => s.townId !== siteConfig.townId);
---
<article class="prose">
  <h1>About {siteConfig.siteName}</h1>

  <p>{siteConfig.siteName} is a local information service for {siteConfig.cityName}, {siteConfig.region}. It is
  being built to bring council and committee papers, events, alerts, travel and weather into one place, updated
  automatically through the day. Every item will link to its original source. The site is at an early stage and
  is not yet open to search engines.</p>

  <h2>How it is made</h2>
  <p>Software checks the sources listed below on a schedule. An AI language model summarises new items, working
  only from what those sources say, and automated checks run before anything publishes. Items go live without a
  person reviewing each one, and no one reports from the ground: every summary is a digest of a public document or
  feed, linked back to the original so you can read the full thing yourself. Read the full, source-by-source
  breakdown on <a href="/how-we-gather-this/">how we gather this</a>.</p>

  <h2>What you won't find here</h2>
  <p>{siteConfig.siteName} does not cover arrests, charges, court proceedings or obituaries. That is a deliberate
  choice, not an oversight: this site exists to tell you what is happening in your city, not to follow anyone
  through a hard moment in their life, and in Scotland reporting on live criminal proceedings carries real legal
  risk.</p>

  <h2>Who built it</h2>
  <p>I am one person, building this in my spare time outside a day job. I am not a journalist and I do not live in
  {siteConfig.cityName}: I write software, from Sweden, and this is what that looks like pointed at local
  information. I would rather say that plainly than dress up an automated site with a fake masthead.</p>
  <p data-qa-allow-sibling-sites>I run the same software pointed at a few other towns too: {siblingSites.map((s, i) => (
    <>{i > 0 && ' and '}<a href={s.siteUrl} rel="noopener">{s.siteName}</a></>
  ))}. Same pipeline, same sourcing rules, same disclosures, just a different place's public records feeding it.
  Saying that plainly here rather than having each site look like an independent operation.</p>

  <h2>Sources</h2>
  {sources.length > 0 ? (
    <>
      <p>Everything on this site traces back to one of these, checked on a schedule and never altered beyond the
      plain-language summary:</p>
      <ul class="sources">
        {sources.map((s) => (
          <li><a href={s.url} rel="noopener">{s.name}</a></li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </>
  ) : (
    <p>No sources are switched on yet. Each one is added only after its availability and terms of use have been
    checked, and appears here when it goes live.</p>
  )}

  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>Automated summaries occasionally get something wrong. Errors are corrected as soon as they are reported:
  <a href="/contact/">use the contact form</a> to flag one.</p>
</article>

<style>
.prose { max-width: 38rem; }
.prose h2 { margin-top: 2.25rem; margin-bottom: 0.75rem; }
.prose p { margin: 0 0 1rem; }
.sources { margin: 0 0 1rem; padding-left: 1.25rem; }
.sources li { margin: 0.25rem 0; }
</style>
